[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m First, we consider the set of divisors of \(9!\) that do not include the primes 2 and 5, as these would result in numbers that are not coprime with 10. Therefore, the relevant divisors are those that are products of the primes 3 and 7. These divisors form the set:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m \[ \{1, 3, 7, 9, 21, 27, 63, 81, 189, 567\} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m We then check which of these divisors are congruent to 1 modulo 10:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m - \(1 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m - \(21 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m - \(81 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m Thus, the divisors of \(9!\) that have a units digit of 1 are 1, 21, and 81. Summing these, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m \[ 1 + 21 + 81 = 103 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m Therefore, the sum of the positive divisors of \(9!\) that have units digit 1 is \(\boxed{103}\).
