[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m First, we factorize \(9!\) as \(362880\), which is \(2^7 \times 3^4 \times 5 \times 7\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m We need to find the divisors of \(9!\) that are congruent to 1 modulo 10. This means the divisors must satisfy \(d \equiv 1 \mod 10\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m Given the prime factorization of \(9!\), we consider the form of the divisors \(d = 2^a \times 3^b \times 5^c \times 7^d\) where \(0 \leq a \leq 7\), \(0 \leq b \leq 4\), \(0 \leq c \leq 1\), and \(0 \leq d \leq 1\). For \(d \equiv 1 \mod 10\), we need \(d \equiv 1 \mod 2\) and \(d \equiv 1 \mod 5\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m We analyze the conditions for \(d \equiv 1 \mod 2\) and \(d \equiv 1 \mod 5\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 1. \(d \equiv 1 \mod 2\) implies \(d\) is odd.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 2. \(d \equiv 1 \mod 5\) implies \(d\) must be congruent to 1 modulo 5.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m Thus, we need to find all divisors \(d\) such that \(d \equiv 1 \mod 5\) and \(d \equiv 1 \mod 2\).
