[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m To compute the sum of the positive divisors of \(9!\) that have units digit 1, we start by factorizing \(9!\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m \[9! = 362880\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m The prime factorization of \(9!\) is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m \[9! = 2^7 \times 3^4 \times 5 \times 7\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m We need to find the divisors of \(9!\) that have units digit 1, which implies the divisors are congruent to 1 modulo 10. These divisors must be odd and their product modulo 10 must be 1.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m We consider the form of the divisors as \(3^b \times 7^d\) with \(c=0\). The conditions for these to be congruent to 1 modulo 10 are analyzed as follows:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m - For \(d=0\), \(3^b \equiv 1 \mod 10\), which happens when \(b \equiv 0 \mod 4\). Thus, \(b = 0\) or \(b = 4\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m - For \(d=1\), \(3^b \times 7 \equiv 1 \mod 10\), which happens when \(b \equiv 1 \mod 4\). Thus, \(b = 1\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m The valid divisors are:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m - \(3^0 \times 7^0 = 1\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m - \(3^4 \times 7^0 = 81\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m - \(3^1 \times 7^1 = 21\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m Summing these divisors:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m \[1 + 21 + 81 = 103\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=455798)[0m Thus, the sum of the positive divisors of \(9!\) that have units digit 1 is:
